------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

An MT81 solar security trailer runs its cameras and its LTE modem off a 24 V
battery pack that a solar array charges during the day. The battery management
system (BMS) is the only thing that knows the real power flow, and the fleet
backend wants two things from every trailer: a live power reading in the console,
and enough history to answer "how many joules did this trailer deliver between
14:05 and 14:07?" when somebody is arguing about runtime.

The BMS vendor gives you this:

    struct BmsSample {
        int      status;      // BMS_OK / BMS_STALE / BMS_ERROR
        float    volts;
        float    amps;        // + = discharging into the load, - = solar charging
        uint64_t timestamp;   // microseconds, same clock as bms_now_us()
    };

    struct BmsSample bms_read(void);

  status        what happens                                    fields
  ------------  ----------------------------------------------  ---------------------
  BMS_OK        blocks until the pack reports, then returns     volts/amps/timestamp
                                                                are valid
  BMS_STALE     blocks, then says "same as last time"           volts/amps/timestamp
                                                                are NOT written
  BMS_ERROR     returns IMMEDIATELY (transport glitch)          every field is garbage

  - The BMS reports when it feels like it: the blocking time is irregular.
  - The FIRST call returns immediately with a valid sample.
  - bms_read() is NOT thread-safe: exactly one thread may be inside it.

The backend upload path is also given:

    int bms_upload(const struct BmsSample *batch, int n);

  Blocks ~3 ms, not thread-safe (one thread only), returns 0 on success. The
  trailer is on a metered LTE plan, so the fleet contract is a hard ceiling:
  at most UPLOAD_RATE_PER_SEC upload calls per second, no bursting around it.

  Scaling for this harness (SPEC §3.1): the real BMS reports about once a
  second; here every BMS interval is divided by ~25, so bms_read() blocks
  20–60 ms (mean ~40 ms) and a whole run takes about 6–7 seconds. The upload rate
  limit (5/s), the batch size, the queue depth and the 30-minute history window
  are NOT scaled — they are in energy_store.h and the harness asserts them.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.  A two-stage pipeline, non-blocking and thread-safe for callers.

    int  bms_pipeline_init(void);
    void bms_pipeline_deinit(void);
    int  bms_get_latest(struct BmsSample *out);   /* 0, or -1 if none yet */
    struct BmsStats bms_stats(void);

  stage 1 (sampler)  owns bms_read() and publishes the latest reading, so that
                     bms_get_latest() never waits for the BMS.
  stage 2 (uploader) consumes samples and uploads them in batches of at most
                     UPLOAD_BATCH_MAX, RATE LIMITED to UPLOAD_RATE_PER_SEC
                     upload calls per second. Implement the limit as a TOKEN
                     BUCKET whose waiting is done with pthread_cond_timedwait()
                     — not usleep(), not a spin, not a fixed sleep slice.
                     (macOS has no pthread_condattr_setclock: see SPEC §6, use
                     pthread_cond_timedwait_relative_np() there.)

  The two stages push against each other: stage 1 produces ~18 samples/s and
  stage 2 may only absorb UPLOAD_RATE_PER_SEC * UPLOAD_BATCH_MAX = 10/s. The
  back-pressure must be absorbed BETWEEN the stages — stage 1 must never slow
  down to stage 2's pace, and must never sit in a lock stage 2 is holding
  across a network call.

  bms_stats() reports sampled / uploaded / dropped / batches / upload_wait_us /
  max_publish_us / stale / errors, and must not block. The harness reads it from
  4 threads while both stages run, and checks that the numbers are consistent
  (nothing invented: sampled >= uploaded + dropped).

Part 2.  Energy over an arbitrary interval, from the last 30 minutes of samples.

    double energy_joules_between(uint64_t t0, uint64_t t1);

  Instantaneous power is p = volts * amps watts; energy is its integral over
  time, in joules. Keep the last ENERGY_WINDOW_US (30 min) of (timestamp, power)
  and answer an arbitrary [t0, t1] in O(log n), not O(n):

    - a ring buffer of samples plus a running PREFIX SUM of trapezoidal energy,
      so whole segments between t0 and t1 cost ONE subtraction;
    - binary search for the segment holding t0 and the segment holding t1;
    - EXACT partial edges: interpolate power at t0 and at t1 instead of
      snapping to the nearest sample. Mind the case where t0 and t1 land inside
      the SAME segment — the prefix-sum difference is 0 there, and the answer
      is not.

  Conventions (energy_store.h spells them out, the harness checks them):
  t1 <= t0 -> 0; fewer than 2 samples -> 0; the query is clamped to the span
  actually measured, so a range entirely outside the retained history -> 0.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour (what main.sh checks; the harness derives every expected
value from what the fake BMS really produced — nothing is hard-coded)

  - a getter called before init, and right after init, behaves (no crash, no
    invented value, no wait on the BMS);
  - counters are consistent: sampled >= uploaded + dropped, every uploaded
    sample is one the BMS really produced, every batch is 1..UPLOAD_BATCH_MAX,
    and no two threads are ever inside the same vendor call;
  - the RATE LIMIT is measured: the harness logs every bms_upload() call and
    asserts that no 1-second window ever holds more than
    UPLOAD_RATE_PER_SEC + UPLOAD_BURST + 1 of them;
  - stage 1 is never blocked: the worst publish (everything except bms_read())
    stays under 10 ms, and samples keep arriving at ~18/s instead of collapsing
    to the uploader's 10/s ceiling, while the uploader is visibly throttled
    (upload_wait_us > 0) and the queue is visibly dropping;
  - energy matches a brute-force reference over the ground truth: whole span,
    sample-aligned ranges, partial edges, both ends inside one segment, a 1 ms
    slice, additivity (E(a,m) + E(m,b) == E(a,b)), and every segment one by one;
  - boundaries: t0 > t1, t0 == t1, empty history, single sample, range before
    the first sample, range after the last sample, range older than the window;
  - 4 reader threads for 2 s: no torn or invented values, energy over a range
    that lies entirely in the past never changes, worst getter latency stays
    far below both the BMS blocking time and one rate-limit interval;
  - shutdown: deinit returns promptly (it must WAKE the timed wait, not wait it
    out), both threads really stop, and the history is still queryable after.

  Last line is exactly "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S) FAILED".

