Things the interviewer is listening for   (answer these on paper before coding)

  1. Why must stage 1 never block on stage 2? Say concretely what is lost when
     the sampler is not sitting inside bms_read() at the moment the BMS reports.
     Why is that different from a slow consumer of a file or a socket?
  2. Who calls bms_read()? Who calls bms_upload()? Why exactly one thread each,
     and how can init take the first sample on the caller's thread without
     breaking that rule?
  3. Why a TIMED wait? Compare it with (a) usleep() in a loop, (b) a separate
     timer thread that signals a plain condvar, (c) spinning on the clock. What
     does each cost you at shutdown, and in wakeups per second?
  4. Token bucket: derive the bound on uploads in any 1-second window. What does
     the bucket's capacity buy you, what does it cost, and what should the bucket
     do after the uploader has been idle for a minute? Why is the capacity not
     "as many tokens as fit"?
  5. The hand-off queue is bounded. Full queue: block, drop the oldest, drop the
     newest, or grow it? Which is right for telemetry and which for a firmware
     download? Why does the choice here cost no energy accuracy at all?
  6. Derive the trapezoid rule for one segment. When is a zero-order hold
     ("power stayed at p_i") the more honest model instead? Why must dt be in
     seconds, and what units does each factor carry?
  7. How much memory does 30 minutes cost, and what worst-case sample rate did
     you assume? Why prefix sums instead of summing the segments on demand, what
     is the query cost, and what must stay true of the buffer for a binary
     search to be legal? The sample in effect at (now - 30 min) is OLDER than
     30 minutes — do you evict it?
  8. The prefix sum grows forever. What breaks if you accumulate it in a float,
     and at roughly what magnitude? Name two ways to fix it (Kahan, rebasing)
     and say why neither is needed here.
  9. BMS_STALE leaves volts/amps/timestamp unwritten and BMS_ERROR leaves every
     field garbage. What must the sampler never do with them, and what does an
     error burst do to your integral — is interpolating across a 2-second hole
     honest? What about a wall-clock jump backwards?
 10. Start-up and shutdown: what does bms_get_latest() return before the first
     sample? deinit() has to return promptly while one thread is inside a 40 ms
     vendor call and the other is parked in a 200 ms timed wait — what exactly
     wakes it, and why must the flag be set before the broadcast, with the lock
     held? What still has to work after deinit?
